t mw-portlet-skin-client-prefs-vector-feature-custom-font-size vector-menu" id="skin-client-prefs-vector-feature-custom-font-size"><div class="vector-menu-heading">Text</div><div class="vector-menu-content"><ul class="vector-menu-content-list"><!-- -3 wrappers  class="mw-list-item mw-list-item-js"--><div class="cdx-radio"><input name="skin-client-pref-vector-feature-custom-font-size-group" id="skin-client-pref-vector-feature-custom-font-size-value-0" type="radio" value="0" data-event-name="skin-client-pref-vector-feature-custom-font-size-value-0" class="cdx-radio__input"><span class="cdx-radio__icon"></span><label class="cdx-label cdx-radio__label"><span class="cdx-label__label__text">Small</span></label></div><!-- -2 siblings --></ul><span class="skin-client-pref-exclusion-notice">This page always uses small font size</span></div></div><div class="mw-portlet mw-portlet-skin-client-prefs-vector-feature-limited-width vector-menu" id="skin-client-prefs-vector-feature-limited-width"><div class="vector-menu-heading">Width</div><div class="vector-menu-content"><ul class="vector-menu-content-list"><!-- -3 wrappers  class="mw-list-item mw-list-item-js"--><div class="cdx-radio"><input name="skin-client-pref-vector-feature-limited-width-group" id="skin-client-pref-vector-feature-limited-width-value-1" type="radio" value="1" data-event-name="skin-client-pref-vector-feature-limited-width-value-1" class="cdx-radio__input"><span class="cdx-radio__icon"></span><label class="cdx-label cdx-radio__label"><span class="cdx-label__label__text">Standard</span></label></div><div class="cdx-radio"><input name="skin-client-pref-vector-feature-limited-width-group" id="skin-client-pref-vector-feature-limited-width-value-0" type="radio" value="0" data-event-name="skin-client-pref-vector-feature-limited-width-value-0" class="cdx-radio__input"><span class="cdx-radio__icon"></span><label class="cdx-label cdx-radio__label"><span class="cdx-label__label__text">Wide</span></label></div></ul><span class="skin-client-pref-exclusion-notice">The content is as wide as possible for your browser window.</span></div></div><div class="mw-portlet mw-portlet-skin-client-prefs-skin-theme vector-menu" id="skin-client-prefs-skin-theme"><div class="vector-menu-heading">Color <span><span></span></span></div><div class="vector-menu-content"><ul class="vector-menu-content-list"><!-- -2 wrappers  class="mw-list-item mw-list-item-js"--><form><div class="cdx-radio"><input name="skin-client-pref-skin-theme-group" id="skin-client-pref-skin-theme-value-os" type="radio" value="os" data-event-name="skin-client-pref-skin-theme-value-os" class="cdx-radio__input"><span class="cdx-radio__icon"></span><label class="cdx-label cdx-radio__label"><span class="cdx-label__label__text">Automatic</span></label></div><!-- -2 siblings --></form><span id="skin-theme-beta-notice"></span></ul><span class="skin-client-pref-exclusion-notice">This page is always in light mode.</span></div></div></nav></div><div id="bodyContent" class="vector-body ve-init-mw-desktopArticleTarget-targetContainer"><div class="vector-body-before-content"><div class="mw-indicators"></div><div id="siteSub" class="noprint">From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia</div></div><div id="contentSub"><!-- -2 wrappers  class="subpages"-->&lt;<bdi><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article">Wikipedia:Today's featured article</a></bdi></div><div id="mw-content-text" class="mw-body-content"><div class="mw-subjectpageheader"></div><div class="mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output"><div class="hlist"><span><!-- -1 wrappers --><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article">Today's featured article</a> archive</span><ul><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/2004">2004</a></li><!-- -10 siblings --></ul><!-- -2 siblings --></div><table class="sidebar nomobile nowraplinks tfarticle-archive" role="navigation"><!-- -3 wrappers  class="sidebar-content"--><div class="navbar plainlinks hlist navbar-mini"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li class="nv-view"><!-- -1 wrappers  title="Template:FApages"--><abbr title="View this template">v</abbr></li><li class="nv-talk"><!-- -1 wrappers  title="Template talk:FApages"--><abbr title="Discuss this template">t</abbr></li><li class="nv-edit"><!-- -1 wrappers  title="Special:EditPage/Template:FApages"--><abbr title="Edit this template">e</abbr></li></div><p><!-- -1 wrappers --><a title="Wikipedia:Featured content">Featured content:</a></p><ul><li><a title="Wikipedia:Featured articles">Featured articles</a> ←</li><!-- -3 siblings --></ul><p><!-- -1 wrappers --><a title="Wikipedia:Featured article candidates">Featured article candidates (FAC):</a></p><ul><li><a title="Wikipedia:Featured article criteria">Featured article criteria</a></li><!-- -2 siblings --></ul><p><!-- -1 wrappers --><a title="Wikipedia:Featured article review">Featured article review (FAR):</a></p><ul><li><a title="Wikipedia:Featured article review/archive">Archived FARs</a></li><!-- -2 siblings --></ul><p><b><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article">Today's featured article (TFA)</a></b>: </p><ul><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/June 2026">This month's queue</a></li><!-- -8 siblings --></ul><p><b>Featured article tools:</b></p><ul><li><a title="Wikipedia:Mentoring for FAC">Mentoring for FAC</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></ul></table><table class="toccolours floatnone"><!-- -1 wrappers --><tr class="calendar-purple"><td><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/April 2026">&lt;&lt;</a></td><th><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Today's featured articles forMay 2026 </a></th><td><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/June 2026">&gt;&gt;</a></td></tr><tr class="calendar-purple"><th title="Sunday">Su </th><!-- -6 siblings --></tr><tr><td></td><!-- -4 siblings --><td><!-- -1 wrappers  title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 1, 2026"--><span>0</span>1</td><td><!-- -1 wrappers  title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 2, 2026"--><span>0</span>2</td></tr><!-- -4 siblings --><tr><td><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 31, 2026">31</a></td><td></td></tr></table><hr><p><b>May 1</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -1 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb"--><span><!-- -1 wrappers  class="mw-file-description" title="Pope Urban II's call for the First Crusade at the Council of Clermont"--><img alt="Pope Urban II's call for the First Crusade at the Council of Clermont" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="1072" data-file-height="1035"></span><div class="thumbcaption"><a title="Pope Urban II">Pope Urban II</a><!-- -2 siblings -->'s call for the at the </div></div><p>The <b><a title="Crusading movement">Crusading movement</a></b> was a major religious, political, and military endeavour of the <a title="Middle Ages">Middle Ages</a><!-- -16 siblings -->, usually dated from the 1095 . There proclaimed the in support of under Muslim rule, framing it as a form of penitential . The crusaders founded in the , the defence of which inspired further . The later launched campaigns against Muslims in , in the Baltic, and other opponents of its authority. Core crusading forces were heavily armed , backed by infantry, local troops, and naval aid from maritime cities. These papal-sanctioned wars fostered distinctive institutions and ideologies. Funding was initially improvised, but later supported via and the sale of . The fusion of chivalric and monastic ideals led to the rise of . The movement extended and left lasting marks on art and literature. <i>(<b><a title="Crusading movement">Fullarticle...</a></b>)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="330 West 42nd Street">330 West 42nd Street</a></li><!-- -2 siblings --></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 1, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 2</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -1 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb"--><span><!-- -1 wrappers  class="mw-file-description" title="Japanese battleship Fuji"--><img alt="Japanese battleship Fuji" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="3732" data-file-height="2632"></span><div class="thumbcaption">Japanese battleship <i>Fuji</i></div></div><p>The <b><!-- -1 wrappers  title="Fuji-class battleship"--><i>Fuji</i> class</b> was a two-ship <a title="Ship class">class</a><!-- -5 siblings --> of built for the (IJN) in the mid-1890s. They were the first battleships in the IJN, and were constructed in the United Kingdom as Japan lacked the industrial facilities needed to build them. The ships participated in the of 1904–1905, including the in February 1904 and two bombardments of the following month. <i><a title="Japanese battleship Yashima">Yashima</a></i> struck a <a title="Naval mine">mine</a> off Port Arthur in May and <a title="Capsizing">capsized</a> while under tow several hours later. <i><a title="Japanese battleship Fuji">Fuji</a></i><i>(pictured)</i> fought in the <a title="Battle of the Yellow Sea">Battle of the Yellow Sea</a><!-- -3 siblings --> and the , and was lightly damaged in the latter action. She was reclassified as a in 1910 and served as a for the rest of her active career. <i>Fuji</i> was <a title="Hulk (ship type)">hulked</a><!-- -2 siblings --> in 1922, converted into a fitted with classrooms, and was finally for scrap in 1948. <i>(<b><a title="Fuji-class battleship">Thisarticle</a></b> is part of a <a title="Wikipedia:Featured topics">featured topic</a>: <b><!-- -1 wrappers  title="Wikipedia:Featured topics/Battleships of Japan"--><i>Battleships of Japan</i></b>.)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Crusading movement">Crusading movement</a></li><!-- -2 siblings --></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 2, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 3</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -1 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb"--><span><!-- -1 wrappers  class="mw-file-description" title="Two characters with a significant age gap"--><img alt="Two characters with a significant age gap" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="2116" data-file-height="2028"></span><div class="thumbcaption">Two characters with a significant age gap</div></div><p>Since the mid-2010s, there has been <a title="Shipping ethics controversy in fanfiction"><b>controversy in the shipping-fanfiction community</b></a>, which discusses <a title="Fan fiction">fan-created</a><!-- -4 siblings --> relationships ("shippings") between fictional characters. This centers on the ethical implications of taboo and abusive content in romantic and sexual pairings between characters. Some fan-created works have depicted disturbing content such as rape, incest, abuse, and pedophilia, often with little connection to the source material. Debate over this material has been prevalent on websites such as and , especially among younger and heavily communities. "Anti-shippers" view such fictional portrayals as normalizing harmful behaviors and posing a threat to children and abuse survivors. "Pro-shippers" reject the notion that such works influence the behaviors of their readers and writers. Anti-shippers have been criticized for spreading attitudes towards sexuality, while pro-shippers face criticism for minimizing all critiques of fan work. <i>(<b><a title="Shipping ethics controversy in fanfiction">Fullarticle...</a></b>)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers  title="Fuji-class battleship"--><i>Fuji</i>-class battleship</li><li><a title="Crusading movement">Crusading movement</a></li><li><a title="330 West 42nd Street">330 West 42nd Street</a></li></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 3, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 4</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -3 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb mw-file-description" title="Maurice Suckling"--><img alt="Maurice Suckling" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="2400" data-file-height="3030"></div><p><b><a title="Maurice Suckling">Maurice Suckling</a></b> (4May 1726– 14July 1778) was a <a title="Royal Navy">Royal Navy</a><!-- -4 siblings --> officer and politician. He saw service in the English Channel and Mediterranean Sea during the . At the start of the , he was promoted to captain and given a command on the . There he played a major part in the , and fought against the French ship <i><a title="French ship Palmier (1752)">Palmier</a></i>. Suckling was employed in the aftermath of the <a title="Capture of Belle Île">capture of Belle Île</a><!-- -9 siblings --> to destroy French fortifications on the and went on at the end of the war. He was given his next command during the in 1770, and took his nephew with him. In 1775, Suckling was appointed as the by , the . Suckling oversaw the Royal Navy's mobilisation when the began. In 1776, he was also elected member of Parliament for . He died unexpectedly in 1778. <i>(<b><a title="Maurice Suckling">Fullarticle...</a></b>)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Shipping ethics controversy in fanfiction">Shipping ethics controversy in fanfiction</a></li><li><!-- -1 wrappers  title="Fuji-class battleship"--><i>Fuji</i>-class battleship</li><li><a title="Crusading movement">Crusading movement</a></li></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 4, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 5</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -1 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb"--><span><!-- -1 wrappers  class="mw-file-description" title="Female katipō"--><img alt="Female katipō" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="2048" data-file-height="1538"></span><div class="thumbcaption">Female katipō</div></div><p>The <b><a title="Katipō">katipō</a></b> (<i>Latrodectus katipo</i>) is a species of <a title="Theridiidae">cobweb spider</a><!-- -4 siblings --> found only in New Zealand. It inhabits close to the seashore and is found on most of New Zealand's coastline, except for the far south and the . In the and the lower half of the , the female has a distinct red stripe bordered in white running down its abdomen <i>(example pictured)</i>; in more northern populations, this stripe is absent or paler. It is most closely related to the Australian <a title="Redback spider">redback spider</a><!-- -4 siblings -->. Like the redback, the katipō is venomous to humans, with its bite being capable of producing the toxic syndrome . Bites are very rare and is available in some hospitals. It mainly feeds on ground-dwelling insects which it catches with an irregular tangled web spun among dune plants. Due to habitat loss, colonisation of their natural habitat by spiders and with the redback spider, the katipō population is regarded as declining. <i>(<b><a title="Katipō">Fullarticle...</a></b>)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Maurice Suckling">Maurice Suckling</a></li><li><a title="Shipping ethics controversy in fanfiction">Shipping ethics controversy in fanfiction</a></li><li><!-- -1 wrappers  title="Fuji-class battleship"--><i>Fuji</i>-class battleship</li></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 5, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 6</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -1 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb"--><span><!-- -1 wrappers  class="mw-file-description" title="Joaquin at peak intensity near the Bahamas"--><img alt="Joaquin at peak intensity near the Bahamas" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="1600" data-file-height="1600"></span><div class="thumbcaption">Joaquin at peak intensity near the Bahamas</div></div><p><b><a title="Hurricane Joaquin">Hurricane Joaquin</a></b> was a powerful <a title="Tropical cyclone">tropical cyclone</a><!-- -12 siblings --> in 2015 and one of the strongest hurricanes on record to affect the Bahamas. It evolved from a to a , becoming a Category4 major hurricane on the on October1. On October3, Joaquin acquired of 155mph (250km/h). Between October1 and 3, it caused extensive damage on , , , , and . Prolonged, intense winds brought down trees and power lines, and unroofed homes. Flooding persisted for days after the hurricane's departure. Relief efforts were hampered by heavy damage to airstrips and flooded roads. The , Cuba, and Haiti were all severely affected. Over the Southeastern United States, drew tremendous moisture from the hurricane, leading to catastrophic flooding in . Over its lifetime, Joaquin killed 34 people and caused US$120million in damage. <i>(<b><a title="Hurricane Joaquin">Fullarticle...</a></b>)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Katipō">Katipō</a></li><!-- -2 siblings --></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 6, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 7</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -3 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb mw-file-description"--><img class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="1330" data-file-height="1862"></div><p><b><a title="Truganini">Truganini</a></b> (<abbr title="circa">c.</abbr><span> 1812</span> – 8 May 1876) was an <a title="Aboriginal Tasmanians">Aboriginal Tasmanian</a><!-- -10 siblings --> woman of the people who was once widely described as the last surviving Aboriginal Tasmanian. She grew up on and saw the death and displacement of much of Tasmania's Aboriginal population during the . She accompanied as a guide on expeditions that resulted in the exile of Tasmania's remaining Aboriginal population; Truganini herself was exiled to the after the expeditions in 1835. She spent a period in the (modern-day ) where she was tried and acquitted for involvement in the murder of two . She was later moved to , where by 1872 she was the only Aboriginal resident left and was mythologised as the last of her race. The narrative that Truganini was the last Aboriginal Tasmanian is rejected by scholars and by the contemporary Aboriginal Tasmanian community. She has become a symbol of what some have characterised as the . <i>(<b><a title="Truganini">Fullarticle...</a></b>)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Hurricane Joaquin">Hurricane Joaquin</a></li><!-- -2 siblings --></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 7, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 8</b></p><p>The <b><a title="First Treaty of London">First Treaty of London</a></b> was formally agreed on 8 May 1358 at <a title="Windsor Castle">Windsor Castle</a><!-- -10 siblings --> in England. Its terms ended the then 21-year-old - conflict now known as the . It was by , and ; the latter was a prisoner, having been captured at the in 1356. The treaty set at four million , the equivalent of the peacetime income of the English Crown for about 20 years. In addition, France was to transfer to England approximately a quarter of its territory, while Edward was to give up his claim to the French throne. The first instalment of the ransom was due to be paid on 1 November, but with the French government collapsing into anarchy it proved impossible to raise. Edward refused to accept less than full payment and so the treaty lapsed. Subsequent negotiations led to the but its terms were so harsh that the French government repudiated it. Hostilities resumed in October 1359, when Edward again invaded France. <i>(<b><a title="First Treaty of London">Fullarticle...</a></b>)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Truganini">Truganini</a></li><!-- -2 siblings --></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 8, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 9</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -1 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb"--><span><!-- -1 wrappers  class="mw-file-description" title="George Krugers in a still from the film"--><img alt="George Krugers in a still from the film" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="1183" data-file-height="1058"></span><div class="thumbcaption">George Krugers in a still from the film</div></div><p><i><!-- -1 wrappers --><a title="The Great Mecca Feast">The Great Mecca Feast</a></i> is a 1928 <a title="Documentary film">documentary film</a> by <a title="George Krugers">George Krugers</a><i>(pictured)</i>. It follows a group of Muslim men from the <a title="Dutch East Indies">Dutch East Indies</a><!-- -4 siblings --> on the pilgrimage, showcasing elements of everyday life and worship in the region of the – including the hajj itself. As was closed to non-Muslims, Krugers passed as a Muslim and recorded film and still-photographic documentation of the pilgrimage. Although it was well-received upon its Dutch premiere on 9November 1928, subsequent showings were rare and the film faded into obscurity. Described as the first documentary about the hajj, the film is the only one of Krugers's works known to have survived. <i>The Great Mecca Feast</i> received scholarly interest in the 2010s. Since then, it has been analysed within the context of colonial networks and control over the hajj process, as well as a primary document providing insight into the experiences of contemporary pilgrims. <i>(<b><a title="The Great Mecca Feast">Fullarticle...</a></b>)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="First Treaty of London">First Treaty of London</a></li><!-- -2 siblings --></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 9, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 10</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -3 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb mw-file-description" title="Ben&Ben"--><img alt="Ben&Ben" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="1920" data-file-height="1080"></div><p><b><a title="Ben&Ben">Ben&amp;Ben</a></b> are a Filipino indie <a title="Folk-pop">folk-pop</a><!-- -4 siblings --> band from . They were formed in 2016 by twin brothers Paolo and Miguel Benjamin Guico (lead vocals and acoustic guitars), calling themselves the Benjamins. A year later, they expanded into an ensemble and settled on the current name, adding Poch Barretto (electric guitar), Keifer Cabugao (violin), Patricia Lasaten (keyboards), Toni Muñoz (percussion), Andrew de Pano (percussion), Agnes Reoma (bass guitar), and Jam Villanueva (drums). The band's musical style has garnered praise for its anthemic quality and emotional engagement that appeals to a broad audience, while their lyrics focus on subjects including loss, heartbreak and relationship, and the journey towards self-love. , including an , an , a <a title="NME Awards"><i>NME</i> Award</a>, two <a title="Myx Music Awards">Myx Music Awards</a><!-- -2 siblings -->, three , and sixteen . In 2020, they placed 29th on the <a title="List of Billboard Social 50 number-one artists"><i>Billboard</i> Social50</a> chart. <i>(<b><a title="Ben&Ben">Thisarticle</a></b> is part of a <a title="Wikipedia:Featured topics">featured topic</a>: <b><!-- -1 wrappers  title="Wikipedia:Featured topics/Overview of Ben&Ben"--><i>Overview of Ben&amp;Ben</i></b>.)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a title="The Great Mecca Feast">The Great Mecca Feast</a></li><!-- -2 siblings --></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 10, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 11</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -1 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb"--><span><!-- -1 wrappers  class="mw-file-description" title="Admire Moon moments away from winning the 2007 race, ahead of Pop Rock and Meisho Samson"--><img alt="Admire Moon moments away from winning the 2007 race, ahead of Pop Rock and Meisho Samson" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="3336" data-file-height="1915"></span><div class="thumbcaption"><a title="Admire Moon">Admire Moon</a><!-- -2 siblings --> moments away from winning the 2007 race, ahead of and </div></div><p>The <b><a title="Japan Cup">Japan Cup</a></b> is a <a title="Group races">Group 1 horse race</a><!-- -3 siblings --> in Japan, held annually on the last Sunday of November at in . It is a race run over a distance of 2400 metres (about <span class="frac">1<span class="sr-only">+</span><span class="num">1</span>⁄<span class="den">2</span></span> miles), with a maximum of 18 horses. First run in 1981, the Japan Cup was created by the <a title="Japan Racing Association">Japan Racing Association</a><!-- -6 siblings --> and extends invitations to top-performing horses aged 3 and above from around the world. The race has had a of over one billion yen since 2023, and is the middle leg of the informal "" along with the and the . The Japan Cup is regularly ranked highly in the ' "Top 100 Group 1 Races of the Year" compilation due to the high quality and depth of racers, and has had winners from all over the world. Initially, the race was dominated by foreign horses, with 8 of the first 10 winners coming from abroad, however, in the last twenty years only from outside Japan has won. <i>(<b><a title="Japan Cup">Fullarticle...</a></b>)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Ben&Ben">Ben&amp;Ben</a></li><!-- -2 siblings --></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 11, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 12</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -1 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb"--><span><!-- -1 wrappers  class="mw-file-description" title="Illustration with Aeneas holding the Golden Bough"--><img alt="Illustration with Aeneas holding the Golden Bough" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="1581" data-file-height="962"></span><div class="thumbcaption">Illustration with Aeneas holding the Golden Bough</div></div><p>The <b><a title="Golden Bough (Aeneid)">Golden Bough</a></b> is a fantastical object in the<i><a title="Aeneid">Aeneid</a></i>, an <a title="Epic poetry">epic poem</a><!-- -9 siblings --> by the 1st century BCE Roman poet . The hero is tasked to find the bough and remove it from its host tree to prove his divine favour before his . It briefly resists as he does so– the implications of which have been widely debated in scholarship. In the medieval period, commentators often interpreted the bough allegorically and as a symbol of wisdom. More recent scholars have viewed the episode as reflecting Virgil's ambivalence towards the Roman Empire, and connected it to the deaths of two of Aeneas's antagonists, and . The bough has been widely referenced in art and literature. It was used by for the title of on comparative religion, is recalled in 's <i><a title="Divine Comedy">Divine Comedy</a></i>, and was the subject of <a title="The Golden Bough (painting)">an 1834 painting</a><!-- -3 siblings --> by . It is also a recurring motif in the "Byzantium" poems of and in the poetry of . <i>(<b><a title="Golden Bough (Aeneid)">Fullarticle...</a></b>)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Japan Cup">Japan Cup</a></li><!-- -2 siblings --></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 12, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 13</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -1 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb"--><span class="skin-invert"><!-- -1 wrappers  class="mw-file-description" title="Logo of Splatoon 3"--><img alt="Logo of Splatoon 3" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="512" data-file-height="278"></span><div class="thumbcaption">Logo of <i>Splatoon3</i></div></div><p><i><!-- -1 wrappers --><a title="Splatoon 3: Side Order">Splatoon3: Side Order</a></i> is an <a title="Expansion pack">expansion pack</a> for the single-player mode of <i><a title="Splatoon 3">Splatoon3</a></i><i>(logo pictured)</i>, a <a title="Video game">video game</a>. It was the second half of the <i>Splatoon3 Expansion Pass</i> and was released on 22February 2024. <i>Side Order</i> follows Agent8, who is trapped in the featureless Memverse. They uncover Order, a rogue <a title="Artificial intelligence">artificial intelligence</a> who threatens to abduct souls to remove their free will and make a world of complete order; Agent8 journeys up a thirty-floor spire to destroy Order. Development began after the release of <i>Splatoon3</i><!-- -3 siblings --> in 2022. The team sought to redo the traditional structure of single-player campaigns, such as having the player's death causing the loss of progress from their current climb, and the option to purchase upgrades to ease future attempts. received favorable reviews, with praise given to its art direction and integration of <span class="nowrap">'s</span> gameplay mechanics into the <a title="Roguelike">roguelike</a> genre. Some critics disliked a perceived lack of level diversity. <i>(<b><a title="Splatoon 3: Side Order">Fullarticle...</a></b>)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers  title="Golden Bough (Aeneid)"-->Golden Bough (<i>Aeneid</i>)</li><li><a title="Japan Cup">Japan Cup</a></li><li><a title="Ben&Ben">Ben&amp;Ben</a></li></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 13, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 14</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -1 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb"--><span><!-- -1 wrappers  class="mw-file-description" title="Locomotive No. 1 Talyllyn arriving at Nant Gwernol"--><img alt="Locomotive No. 1 Talyllyn arriving at Nant Gwernol" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="3888" data-file-height="2592"></span><div class="thumbcaption">Locomotive No.1 <i><a title="Talyllyn (locomotive)">Talyllyn</a></i> arriving at Nant Gwernol</div></div><p>The <b><a title="Talyllyn Railway">Talyllyn Railway</a></b> is a <a title="Narrow-gauge railway">narrow-gauge</a><!-- -10 siblings --> preserved railway in Wales running for 7.25 miles (11.67km) from on the coast to near the village of . The line was opened in 1866 to carry from the quarries at to Tywyn, and was the first narrow-gauge railway in Britain authorised by to carry passengers using steam haulage. Despite severe under-investment, the line remained open, and on 14May 1951 it became the first railway in the world to be operated as a by volunteers. Since preservation, the railway has operated as a , significantly expanding its rolling stock through acquisition and an engineering programme to build new locomotives and carriages. The fictional , which formed part of the <i><a title="The Railway Series">Railway Series</a></i> of children's books by the <a title="Wilbert Awdry">Rev. W Awdry</a>, was based on the Talyllyn Railway. The preservation of the line inspired the <a title="Ealing comedies">Ealing comedy</a> film <i><a title="The Titfield Thunderbolt">The Titfield Thunderbolt</a></i>. <i>(<b><a title="Talyllyn Railway">Fullarticle...</a></b>)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a title="Splatoon 3: Side Order">Splatoon3: Side Order</a></li><li><!-- -1 wrappers  title="Golden Bough (Aeneid)"-->Golden Bough (<i>Aeneid</i>)</li><li><a title="Japan Cup">Japan Cup</a></li></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 14, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 15</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -1 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb"--><span><!-- -1 wrappers  class="mw-file-description" title="Contested area during Operation Brevity"--><img alt="Contested area during Operation Brevity" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="438" data-file-height="457"></span><div class="thumbcaption">Contested area during Operation Brevity</div></div><p><b><a title="Operation Brevity">Operation Brevity</a></b> was an offensive conducted in May1941, during the <a title="Western Desert campaign">Western Desert campaign</a><!-- -4 siblings --> of the Second World War, against front-line forces in the –– area of the border between Egypt and Libya <i>(map pictured)</i>. British <a title="Middle East Command">Middle East Command</a><!-- -7 siblings --> general defined Operation Brevity's main goals as the acquisition of territory from which to launch a further planned offensive toward . On 15May, Brigadier attacked in three columns with a mixed infantry and armoured force. The was taken against stiff Italian opposition, and deeper inside Libya was captured, but German counter-attacks under Colonel regained the fort during the afternoon. Gott conducted a staged withdrawal to the Halfaya Pass on 16May, and Operation Brevity ended. The Halfaya Pass was recaptured 11 days later during , a German counter-attack. <i>(<b><a title="Operation Brevity">Fullarticle...</a></b>)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Talyllyn Railway">Talyllyn Railway</a></li><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a title="Splatoon 3: Side Order">Splatoon3: Side Order</a></li><li><!-- -1 wrappers  title="Golden Bough (Aeneid)"-->Golden Bough (<i>Aeneid</i>)</li></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 15, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 16</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -1 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb"--><span><!-- -1 wrappers  class="mw-file-description" title="Richard Harmon, who portrayed Erik Campbell"--><img alt="Richard Harmon, who portrayed Erik Campbell" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="1667" data-file-height="2327"></span><div class="thumbcaption">Richard Harmon, who portrayed Erik Campbell</div></div><p><b><a title="Erik Campbell (Final Destination)">Erik Campbell</a></b> is a character in <i><a title="Final Destination Bloodlines">Final Destination Bloodlines</a></i> (2025), the sixth film in the <a title="Supernatural horror film">supernatural horror</a> franchise <i><a title="Final Destination">Final Destination</a></i>, and was portrayed by <a title="Richard Harmon">Richard Harmon</a><i>(pictured)</i>. Introduced as a grandson of Iris Campbell, who escaped Death in the 1960s, Erik is revealed to have been conceived out of his mother's affair and thus not in any danger. Despite this, while trying to help his brother cheat Death, Erik is sucked into an <a title="Magnetic resonance imaging">MRI</a> machine by a wheelchair that crushes and impales him. The reveal of Erik's parentage was due to the film's crew wanting to subvert the audience's expectations regarding the order of deaths; a discarded idea involved twins. The directors were initially apprehensive about incorporating an MRI-machine death, but chose to include it due to positive feedback from the production team. Critics responded positively to Erik, describing him as a fan favorite and <i>Bloodlines</i><span class="nowrap">'</span> best character, as well as praising his death scene and Harmon's performance. <i>(<b><a title="Erik Campbell (Final Destination)">Fullarticle...</a></b>)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Operation Brevity">Operation Brevity</a></li><!-- -2 siblings --></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 16, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 17</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -1 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb"--><span><!-- -1 wrappers  class="mw-file-description" title="Shannen Doherty"--><img alt="Shannen Doherty" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="1146" data-file-height="1479"></span><div class="thumbcaption">Shannen Doherty</div></div><p>"<b><a title="All Hell Breaks Loose (Charmed)">All Hell Breaks Loose</a></b>" is the third-season finale of <i><a title="Charmed">Charmed</a></i>, an American <a title="Fantasy">fantasy</a><!-- -8 siblings --> series that aired on . It follows (), (), and (), three sisters who discover they are witches and use their powers to protect innocents from demons. "All Hell Breaks Loose" was written by and directed by Doherty <i>(pictured)</i>, and aired on May17, 2001. It was the third episode of the series directed by Doherty. In the episode, Prue and Piper are caught using their powers on live television, which proves to have deadly consequences. During filming, Doherty used a <a title="Salvador Dalí">Salvador Dalí</a> painting as inspiration for the episode's aesthetic, and helped her co-stars shoot emotionally challenging scenes. A week prior to the episode's airing, Doherty was fired from the series due to a feud with Milano, which resulted in her character being killed off. "All Hell Breaks Loose" has been cited as one of the show's best episodes, with critics highlighting Prue's death. <i>(<b><a title="All Hell Breaks Loose (Charmed)">Fullarticle...</a></b>)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers  title="Erik Campbell (Final Destination)"-->Erik Campbell (<i>Final Destination</i>)</li><li><a title="Operation Brevity">Operation Brevity</a></li><li><a title="Talyllyn Railway">Talyllyn Railway</a></li></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 17, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 18</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -3 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb mw-file-description" title="Sursock bronze"--><img alt="Sursock bronze" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="2472" data-file-height="4120"></div><p>The <b><a title="Sursock bronze">Sursock bronze</a></b> is a <a title="Gilding">gilded</a><!-- -2 siblings --> bronze sculptural group of dating to the 2nd century AD. A miniature of the cult statue that stood in the , Lebanon, it depicts the god as a beardless youth wearing a <i><a title="Calathus (basket)">kalathos</a></i>, a basket-shaped headdress, and an <i><a title="Ependytes">ependytes</a></i>, a close-fitting dress, under ornate armor. The front of the armor bears busts of seven deities associated with celestial bodies—<a title="Sol (Roman mythology)">Sol</a><!-- -12 siblings -->, , , , , (replacing ), and —arranged in an order encoding both the Chaldean sequence of planets and the days of the . The piece illustrates the of Canaanite, Greek, and Roman traditions, tracing the evolution of Heliopolitan Jupiter from the Canaanite storm god into a cosmic deity of planetary order and prophecy. Named after its former owner, the Beiruti aristocrat , and acquired by the in 1939, the piece inaugurated the first issue of <i><a title="Syria (journal)">Syria</a></i>, the leading French journal of Levantine archaeology, in 1920. <i>(<b><a title="Sursock bronze">Fullarticle...</a></b>)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers  title="All Hell Breaks Loose (Charmed)"-->"All Hell Breaks Loose" (<i>Charmed</i>)</li><li><!-- -1 wrappers  title="Erik Campbell (Final Destination)"-->Erik Campbell (<i>Final Destination</i>)</li><li><a title="Operation Brevity">Operation Brevity</a></li></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 18, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 19</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -1 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb"--><span><!-- -1 wrappers  class="mw-file-description" title="Approximate extent of the Silverthrone Caldera"--><img alt="Approximate extent of the Silverthrone Caldera" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="850" data-file-height="687"></span><div class="thumbcaption">Approximate extent of the Silverthrone Caldera</div></div><p>The <b><a title="Silverthrone Caldera">Silverthrone Caldera</a></b> is a poorly studied volcano in the Range2 Coast Land District of <a title="British Columbia">British Columbia</a><!-- -10 siblings -->, Canada. It lies within the of the and reaches an elevation of 2,860 metres (9,380 feet), although some sources give an elevation as high as 3,160m (10,370ft). Deeply eroded, the caldera is about 25 by 20 kilometres (16 by 12 miles) in size and has a rugged topography. The area is the origin of several streams and contains several named , including . Volcanic rocks deposited by eruptions include , , and . They are exposed in valleys, but at higher elevations they are largely buried under glacial ice. The Silverthrone Caldera was a source of for indigenous peoples during the . Geological studies have been conducted at the volcano since at least the 1960s, but its very remote location has impeded detailed fieldwork. <i>(<b><a title="Silverthrone Caldera">Fullarticle...</a></b>)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Sursock bronze">Sursock bronze</a></li><li><!-- -1 wrappers  title="All Hell Breaks Loose (Charmed)"-->"All Hell Breaks Loose" (<i>Charmed</i>)</li><li><a title="Erik Campbell (Final Destination)">Erik Campbell (Final Destination)</a></li></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 19, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 20</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -1 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb"--><span><!-- -1 wrappers  class="mw-file-description" title="Continental Army riflemen skirmishing with enemy troops at Saratoga"--><img alt="Continental Army riflemen skirmishing with enemy troops at Saratoga" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="1926" data-file-height="1407"></span><div class="thumbcaption">Continental Army riflemen skirmishing with enemy troops <a title="Battles of Saratoga">at Saratoga</a></div></div><p>The <b><a title="Maryland and Virginia Rifle Regiment">Maryland and Virginia Rifle Regiment</a></b> was organized in June1776 as a <a title="Light infantry">light infantry</a><!-- -12 siblings --> unit of riflemen in the during the . The consisted of nine – four from Maryland and five from Virginia– and was directly responsible to national authority as an . Most of the regiment surrendered to British and German forces at the on November16, 1776. However, a portion of the unit continued to serve actively in the Continental Army throughout most of the remainder of the war. Elements of the regiment served with 's main army and participated in its major engagements. Select members of the unit were also attached to Colonel 's elite . The regiment was reorganized in January1779 and stationed at , headquarters of the Continental Army's , in present-day western . Disbanded in January1781, it was the longest serving Continental Army rifle unit of the war. <i>(<b><a title="Maryland and Virginia Rifle Regiment">Fullarticle...</a></b>)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Silverthrone Caldera">Silverthrone Caldera</a></li><li><a title="Sursock bronze">Sursock bronze</a></li><li><!-- -1 wrappers  title="All Hell Breaks Loose (Charmed)"-->"All Hell Breaks Loose" <i>(Charmed)</i></li></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 20, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 21</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -3 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb mw-file-description" title="2006 Football League Championship play-off final"--><img alt="2006 Football League Championship play-off final" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="1152" data-file-height="864"></div><p>The <b><a title="2006 Football League Championship play-off final">2006 Football League Championship play-off final</a></b> was an <a title="Association football">association football</a><!-- -8 siblings --> match played on 21May 2006 at the in , Wales, between and . The match was to determine the third and final team to gain from the , the second tier of , to the . The winners of two semi-finals competed for the final Premiership place of the <a title="2006–07 FA Premier League"><span class="nowrap">2006–07</span> season</a>. Winning the final was estimated to be worth up to £40million to the successful team. The match was watched by a crowd of 64,736. It was the last <a title="EFL Championship play-offs">play-off</a><!-- -2 siblings --> final to be held at the Millennium Stadium, as the new was completed in time for the . Watford won the match <span class="nowrap">3–0</span>, with opening goalscorer <a title="Jay DeMerit">Jay DeMerit</a><!-- -3 siblings --> named . Leeds goalkeeper scored an to make the score <span class="nowrap">2–0</span> to Watford after 60 minutes, and the final goal was a <a title="Penalty kick (association football)">penalty kick</a> scored by <a title="Darius Henderson">Darius Henderson</a>. <i>(<b><a title="2006 Football League Championship play-off final">Fullarticle...</a></b>)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Maryland and Virginia Rifle Regiment">Maryland and Virginia Rifle Regiment</a></li><!-- -2 siblings --></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 21, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 22</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -1 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb"--><span><!-- -1 wrappers  class="mw-file-description" title="Proboscis of a male Intraproboscis sanghae"--><img alt="Proboscis of a male Intraproboscis sanghae" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="1092" data-file-height="489"></span><div class="thumbcaption">Proboscis of a male <i>Intraproboscis sanghae</i></div></div><p><i><!-- -1 wrappers --><a title="Intraproboscis">Intraproboscis</a></i> is a genus in <a title="Acanthocephala">Acanthocephala</a><!-- -6 siblings --> (thorny-headed or spiny-headed worms) containing a single species. Found in Central Africa, it infests the and the , which are both threatened with . Female worms reach up to 180mm (7.1in) long (mostly ) and 2mm (0.079in) wide; males seem to be smaller. The body consists of a long, narrow trunk and a tubular covered with hooks <i>(pictured)</i>, used for feeding and attachment. The life cycle of <i>I.sanghae</i> remains unknown but it likely involves a complex <a title="Biological life cycle">life cycle</a><!-- -3 siblings --> with at least two hosts, the pangolin and one believed to be an , such as an insect. Within this host, the develop into an infectious stage called a cystacanth. When a consumes the intermediate host, the cystacanths enter the intestines where they mature. The worm's eggs are expelled and hatch into new larvae. Infestation by <i>I.sanghae</i> can cause <a title="Gastrointestinal perforation">intestinal perforation</a> and death in the black-bellied pangolin. <i>(<b><a title="Intraproboscis">Fullarticle...</a></b>)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="2006 Football League Championship play-off final">2006 Football League Championship play-off final</a></li><!-- -2 siblings --></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 22, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 23</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -1 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb"--><span><!-- -1 wrappers  class="mw-file-description" title="Paramore performing during the This Is Why Tour"--><img alt="Paramore performing during the This Is Why Tour" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="5419" data-file-height="3044"></span><div class="thumbcaption">Paramore performing during the <a title="This Is Why Tour">This Is Why Tour</a></div></div><p>"<b><a title="Running Out of Time (song)">Running Out of Time</a></b>" is a song by the American rock band <a title="Paramore">Paramore</a><i>(pictured)</i>. It was released on May23, 2023, by <a title="Atlantic Records">Atlantic Records</a> as the fourth single from the band's sixth studio album <i><a title="This Is Why">This Is Why</a></i> (2023). <a title="Hayley Williams">Hayley Williams</a><!-- -4 siblings -->, , and wrote the song in the early stages of the album's production, and produced it. The band premiered the song at a concert at the on February7, 2023, and performed it on <i><a title="Jimmy Kimmel Live!">Jimmy Kimmel Live!</a></i> on February14. Described by critics as a <a title="Pop rock">pop-rock</a><!-- -2 siblings --> and song, "Running Out of Time" deals with time management and comments on social anxieties over death, aging, and losing friends. Williams said the song was influenced by her personal struggle with punctuality and her friendship with . Critics positively reviewed the song, praising its lyrics as playful and its composition as well-balanced. The song peaked on several international charts in 2023 and ranked on <i>Billboard</i><span class="nowrap">'s</span> year-end <a title="Alternative Airplay">Alternative Airplay</a> chart. <i>(<b><a title="Running Out of Time (song)">Fullarticle...</a></b>)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a title="Intraproboscis">Intraproboscis</a></li><!-- -2 siblings --></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 23, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 24</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -1 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb"--><span><!-- -1 wrappers  class="mw-file-description" title="Capital Indoor Stadium"--><img alt="Capital Indoor Stadium" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="6013" data-file-height="4009"></span><div class="thumbcaption">Capital Indoor Stadium</div></div><p>The <b><a title="Figure skating at the 2022 Winter Olympics – Team event">figure skating team event at the 2022 Winter Olympics</a></b> was held between 4 and 7February at the <a title="Capital Indoor Stadium">Capital Indoor Stadium</a><i>(pictured)</i> in <a title="Beijing">Beijing</a><!-- -9 siblings -->, China. It consisted of competitions in , , and . Skaters and teams earned points based on their placements in each event; medalists were determined based on their total points. The team representing the originally finished in first place, while the team from the United States finished second, and the Japanese team third. The medal ceremony was postponed when a was received for of Russia. After nearly two years of litigation, Valieva's scores were stricken and the newly tabulated results placed the American team first, the Japanese team second, and the Russian team third. The American and Japanese teams received their medals at a ceremony held at the in during the . <i>(<b><a title="Figure skating at the 2022 Winter Olympics – Team event">Fullarticle...</a></b>)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Running Out of Time (song)">"Running Out of Time" (song)</a></li><!-- -2 siblings --></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 24, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 25</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -1 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb"--><span><!-- -1 wrappers  class="mw-file-description" title="Jewish head coverings called kippot"--><img alt="Jewish head coverings called kippot" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="3648" data-file-height="2736"></span><div class="thumbcaption">Jewish head coverings called <span title="Hebrew-language text"><i>kippot</i></span></div></div><p><i><!-- -1 wrappers --><a title="Menora v. Illinois High School Association">Menora v. Illinois High School Association</a></i> is a case heard by the <a title="United States Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit">U.S. Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit</a><!-- -2 siblings --> which centered on two which sued the (IHSA) when the IHSA would not let them compete in a basketball tournament unless student-athletes removed their <span title="Hebrew-language text"><!-- -1 wrappers --><a title="Kippah">kippot</a></span><i>(examples pictured)</i> from their heads during play. The schools argued that their <a title="First Amendment to the United States Constitution">First Amendment</a> right of <a title="Freedom of religion in the United States">freedom of religion</a> was violated. The IHSA stated that banning the <span title="Hebrew-language text"><i>kippot</i></span> was reasonable because they could fall off during play, risking player injury. The Seventh Circuit held that no conflict would exist if the schools designed a head covering that was not a safety risk. The case was settled in June1983, allowing <span title="Hebrew-language text"><i>kippot</i></span> to be worn with <a title="Hair clip">contour clips</a>. Legal scholars criticized the Seventh Circuit's false conflict approach in the decision as unsupported by precedent. American Jewish communities largely took it as a win that the students were allowed to play with <span title="Hebrew-language text"><i>kippot</i></span> on. <i>(<b><a title="Menora v. Illinois High School Association">Fullarticle...</a></b>)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Figure skating at the 2022 Winter Olympics – Team event">Figure skating at the 2022 Winter Olympics – Team event</a></li><!-- -2 siblings --></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 25, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 26</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -3 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb mw-file-description" title="Sally Ride"--><img alt="Sally Ride" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="2790" data-file-height="3487"></div><p><b><a title="Sally Ride">Sally Ride</a></b> (May26, 1951– July23, 2012) was an American astronaut and physicist. Born in Los Angeles, she joined <a title="NASA">NASA</a><!-- -3 siblings --> in 1978, and in 1983 became the third woman and the first American woman to fly in space. A graduate of , where she earned a PhD in physics in 1978, she was selected as a with , the first class of NASA astronauts to include women. She flew in space on the <a title="Space Shuttle Challenger">Space Shuttle <i>Challenger</i></a> on the <a title="STS-7"><span class="nowrap">STS-7</span></a> mission in 1983 and the <a title="STS-41-G"><span class="nowrap">STS-41-G</span></a> mission in 1984. She left NASA in 1987 and worked at Stanford's <a title="Stanford University centers and institutes">Center for International Security and Arms Control</a><!-- -3 siblings -->, and then at the , researching and . She served on the committees that investigated the <a title="Space Shuttle Challenger disaster">losses of <i>Challenger</i></a> and <a title="Space Shuttle Columbia disaster">of <i>Columbia</i></a>, and was the only person to participate in both investigations. She is the first astronaut known to have been LGBTQ, a fact that she hid until her death from <a title="Pancreatic cancer">pancreatic cancer</a> in 2012. <i>(<b><a title="Sally Ride">Fullarticle...</a></b>)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a title="Menora v. Illinois High School Association">Menora v. Illinois High School Association</a></li><!-- -2 siblings --></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 26, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 27</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -1 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb"--><span><!-- -1 wrappers  class="mw-file-description" title="Flag of the Nation of Islam"--><img alt="Flag of the Nation of Islam" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="900" data-file-height="600"></span><div class="thumbcaption">Flag of the Nation of Islam</div></div><p>The <b><a title="Nation of Islam">Nation of Islam</a></b> (NOI) is a religious organization founded in the United States by <a title="Wallace Fard Muhammad">Wallace Fard Muhammad</a><!-- -10 siblings --> in 1930. It is centralized, hierarchical, and committed to ; membership is open only to . Practitioners are expected to live disciplined lives, adhering to patriarchal gender roles, strict dress codes and specific dietary requirements. The NOI campaigns for the creation of an independent nation-state and calls for African Americans to be economically self-sufficient. In 2007, it was estimated to have 50,000 members. While describing itself as Islamic and using Islamic terminology, its religious tenets differ substantially from orthodox . characterize it as a . Muslim critics accuse the NOI of promoting teachings that are not authentically Islamic. Other critics have characterized it as a that , , and . <i>(<b><a title="Nation of Islam">Fullarticle...</a></b>)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Sally Ride">Sally Ride</a></li><!-- -2 siblings --></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 27, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 28</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -3 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb mw-file-description" title="Physella acuta"--><img alt="Physella acuta" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="3893" data-file-height="2920"></div><p><i><!-- -1 wrappers --><a title="Physella acuta">Physella acuta</a></i> is a <a title="Species">species</a><!-- -2 siblings --> of small, air-breathing in the family of North American origin. Like other physids, <i>P.acuta</i> presents a sinistral (left-coiling) shell as well as a unique set of muscles called the physid musculature that allows it to rapidly twist the shell as a <a title="Anti-predator adaptation">defence mechanism</a>. <i>Physellaacuta</i> is <a title="Invasive species">invasive</a><!-- -5 siblings --> outside its native range. Its first introduction outside North America likely occurred through the to Europe, with later spread mainly through the . The species can occupy diverse freshwater habitats and tolerates polluted as well as environments. Due to its high reproductive rate and tolerance to , it frequently native snail species. In aquariums, <i>P.acuta</i> is usually introduced through <a title="Ornamental plant">ornamental plants</a> and can become a "nuisance snail" due to its rapid reproduction. However, a controlled population can help clean up organic leftovers and control algae. <i>(<b><a title="Physella acuta">Fullarticle...</a></b>)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Nation of Islam">Nation of Islam</a></li><!-- -2 siblings --></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 28, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 29</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -3 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb mw-file-description" title="Patrick Henry"--><img alt="Patrick Henry" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="610" data-file-height="737"></div><p><b><a title="Patrick Henry">Patrick Henry</a></b> (May29, 1736– June6, 1799) was an American attorney known for his declaration to the <a title="Virginia Conventions">Second Virginia Convention</a><!-- -11 siblings --> in 1775: "" Beginning a law practice in 1760, he soon became prominent through his victory in the . Henry was elected to the Virginia , where he quickly became notable for his inflammatory rhetoric against the . In 1774 and 1775, Henry served as a delegate to the and . Back in , Henry urged independence, and when this was declared, served as until 1779, and then in the until he began his last two terms as governor in 1784. Henry feared a strong federal government, and he actively opposed the ratification of the . A slaveholder throughout his adult life, he hoped to see slavery end, but had no plan to accomplish that. Henry is remembered for his oratory, and as a . <i>(<b><a title="Patrick Henry">Fullarticle...</a></b>)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a title="Physella acuta">Physella acuta</a></li><!-- -2 siblings --></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 29, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 30</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -3 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb mw-file-description" title="Serpent labret with articulated tongue"--><img alt="Serpent labret with articulated tongue" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="990" data-file-height="743"></div><p>The <b><a title="Serpent labret with articulated tongue">serpent labret with articulated tongue</a></b> is a gold-alloy <a title="Body piercing jewelry">body ornament</a> from the <a title="Aztecs">Aztec</a> culture of the mid-second millennium. Designed to be inserted into a piercing below the lower lip, it depicts a fanged serpent poised to strike, with a bifurcated tongue hanging from its mouth. The tongue, which is moveable and retractable <i>(animation pictured)</i>, would have swung from side to side with its wearer's movements. Art historians have described it as among the finest of the fewer than 400 Aztec gold objects known to survive. The labret is 6.7cm (2.6in) high, 6.7cm (2.6in) long, and weighs 51 grams (1.81oz). Consisting of a gold, copper, and silver alloy, it was made by <a title="Lost-wax casting">lost-wax casting</a><!-- -4 siblings -->. , or lip plugs, were associated with eloquence and nobility in Aztec culture, while gold was a hallmark of divinity. The serpent may represent , the fire serpent wielded as a weapon by the sun god . The labret was purchased in 2016 by the . <i>(<b><a title="Serpent labret with articulated tongue">Fullarticle...</a></b>)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Patrick Henry">Patrick Henry</a></li><!-- -2 siblings --></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 30, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div><hr><p><b>May 31</b></p><div id="mp-tfa-img"><!-- -3 wrappers  class="thumbinner mp-thumb mw-file-description" title="Bicentennial Capitol Mall State Park"--><img alt="Bicentennial Capitol Mall State Park" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="2250" data-file-height="1500"></div><p>The <b><a title="Bicentennial Capitol Mall State Park">Bicentennial Capitol Mall State Park</a></b> is an urban <a title="Linear park">linear park</a><!-- -16 siblings --> in downtown , United States. The park is 19 acres (7.7 hectares) in size and adjoins the grounds of the . It is modeled on the in , and incorporates , , and . It uses to showcase the , , , and through a series of monuments, walkways, and interpretive displays. Receiving more than 2.5million visitors annually, it is the most visited of the 57 . The park was designed by Tuck Hinton Architects in 1992 and 1993, groundbreaking occurred on June27, 1994, and the park was dedicated on June1, 1996, the 200th anniversary of 's statehood. Since then, the and the have moved to the park, which is now recognized as a cultural and historical landmark. <i>(<b><a title="Bicentennial Capitol Mall State Park">Fullarticle...</a></b>)</i></p><div class="tfa-recent"> Recently featured: <div class="hlist inline"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Serpent labret with articulated tongue">Serpent labret with articulated tongue</a></li><!-- -2 siblings --></div></div><div class="hlist tfa-footer noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><!-- -1 wrappers --><a class="mw-selflink selflink">Archive</a></li><!-- -3 siblings --></div><div class="hlist plainlinks metadata noprint"><!-- -1 wrappers --><li><a title="Wikipedia:Today's featured article/May 31, 2026">view</a></li><!-- -4 siblings --></div><div></div></div><div class="printfooter">Retrieved from "<a>https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Wikipedia:Today%27s_featured_article/May_2026&amp;oldid=1317099623</a>"</div></div><div id="catlinks" class="catlinks"><!-- -1 wrappers  class="mw-normal-catlinks"--><a title="Help:Category">Category</a>: <ul><!-- -1 wrappers --><a title="Category:Wikipedia Today's featured article archives">Wikipedia Today's featured article archives</a></ul></div></div></div><div class="mw-footer-container"><!-- -1 wrappers  class="mw-footer" id="footer"--><ul id="footer-info"><li id="footer-info-lastmod"> This page was last edited on 16 October 2025, at 09:26<span class="anonymous-show">(UTC)</span>.</li><li id="footer-info-copyright">Text is available under the <a title="Wikipedia:Text of the Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International License">Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 License</a>; additional terms may apply. By using this site, you agree to the <a class="extiw" title="foundation:Special:MyLanguage/Policy:Terms of Use">Terms of Use</a> and <a class="extiw" title="